# Runtime environment name — defaults to the current git branch (main → production).
# Set explicitly in environments without a git checkout (Docker, CI).
ENVIRONMENT=development

# Required
DISCORD_TOKEN=your_discord_bot_token
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# Optional — Spotify link support. Set BOTH to enable playing Spotify tracks and
# playlists; leave both unset (or blank) to run without Spotify (YouTube, SoundCloud,
# other yt-dlp sites, and search still work). On startup the bot validates these
# credentials against the Spotify API (fetching a known track). If they're rejected,
# it logs an error and disables Spotify links — playback of other sources is
# unaffected, and `-ping` reports the current Spotify status.
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=your_spotify_client_id
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=your_spotify_client_secret

# Postgres play-history archive (docs/POSTGRES_HISTORY_PLAN.md) — OPT-IN.
# By default NOTHING is written to long-term storage: plays live only in the
# capped per-guild Redis list behind -history (50 entries), and a default
# `docker compose up` starts no Postgres at all. Opting in means permanently
# recording every play (guild id, user id, title, timestamp) until you delete
# the data — a choice the deployer makes, never a side effect.
#
# UPGRADING AN EXISTING INSTALL? The 50-entry cap is NOT opt-in and is applied
# in BOTH modes, on every song end. Builds before 2.5.0 never trimmed that
# list, so each guild loses everything beyond its newest 50 plays at its next
# song end — silently, with nothing to undo. If Postgres is not already
# holding a copy, back Redis up BEFORE deploying: see "Upgrading to 2.5.0" in
# README.md. `just db-backfill` is the preservation path only if you are
# opting in; it refuses to run without a reachable database.
#
# Enable it with ONE line, then deploy through the tooling:
# HISTORY_ARCHIVE_ENABLED=true
#
#   just up          (or ./build_docker.sh, or ./deploy_docker.sh)
#
# Those derive the `archive` compose profile from the flag and bring up Postgres
# and the schema one-shot with the bot. A RAW `docker compose up` cannot: Compose
# activates profiles only from COMPOSE_PROFILES or --profile, and never reads
# this variable — so it deploys the default stack and no Postgres. That is the
# documented split, not an oversight. To drive compose directly anyway, either
# name the services (`docker compose up -d postgres db-migrate`) or export the
# profile yourself (`COMPOSE_PROFILES=archive`); both still work.
#
# Disabling it later: `just down` FIRST (it always passes --profile archive, so
# it stops Postgres in either state), then set the line to false, then `just up`.
# Disabling stops collection; it does NOT erase.
# Already-archived rows stay in the postgres-data volume until you remove it:
#   docker volume rm discord-music-bot_postgres-data
# is the erasure step. A startup warning will report any plays that were
# buffered but never archived; `just outbox` inspects, `DEL history:outbox`
# discards.
#
# Everything below this point matters only while the archive is ENABLED.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD falls back to "password" when unset, so an archive-enabled
# `docker compose up` works with nothing else configured. That default is a
# first-run convenience, not a setting to keep: while it is in use the bot
# logs an ERROR at every startup and renders a warning embed on every -ping.
#
# Run ./setup_env.sh to replace it with a fresh 128-bit random value —
# independent of every other secret, and deliberately NOT derived from
# DISCORD_TOKEN, so rotating the token cannot invalidate a live database
# credential. USER and DB default to "musicbot" if left unset.
#
# CHANGING IT LATER TAKES THREE STEPS, IN ORDER. Postgres reads this variable
# only when it initializes an EMPTY data directory, so an existing postgres-data
# volume keeps whatever password it was created with:
#   1. ALTER USER <user> PASSWORD '<new>'   (the server itself)
#   2. edit this file to match
#   3. docker compose up -d                 (the bot's DSN is baked at create)
# The order matters — the bot's warning reads its DSN, so doing (2) first
# silences it while the database still accepts the old password.
# To start clean instead, drop ONLY the database volume:
#   docker compose down && docker volume rm discord-music-bot_postgres-data
# NOT `down -v`: that also removes redis-data, which holds plays not yet durable
# in Postgres — the outbox is the only copy those have.
# POSTGRES_USER=musicbot
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# POSTGRES_DB=musicbot
#
# Host port the bundled Postgres publishes on (loopback only). The bot uses host
# networking, so it connects through this port — change it if something else on
# your machine already owns 5432.
# POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5432
#
# Running Postgres yourself instead of the compose service? Set the URL here and
# that is all — compose builds its DSN as ${POSTGRES_URL:-<parts>}, so yours wins
# with no edit to any tracked file. Remember to run `just db-migrate` against it:
# the bot verifies the schema version and never creates it.
# POSTGRES_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname
#
# DSN for `just db-migrate` only, so the migrating role can hold DDL rights the
# bot's role does not. Falls back to POSTGRES_URL.
# POSTGRES_MIGRATE_URL=postgresql://ddl_user:password@host:5432/dbname
#
# Ceiling on the play-history outbox, in entries — meaningful only while the
# archive is enabled (disabled, the outbox is never written). 0 (the default)
# means UNBOUNDED, and that is the durability contract: an entry only leaves
# the outbox once Postgres has it. Setting a cap is choosing to DISCARD the
# oldest un-archived plays — real, unrecoverable data loss — in exchange for
# bounding a non-evictable Redis key during a long Postgres outage. Every drop
# logs at ERROR. Must be >= 0; a negative value is refused at startup (it
# would read as an active cap and empty the outbox on every drain).
# HISTORY_OUTBOX_MAX=0
#
# asyncpg prepared-statement cache size, per connection. Set to 0 behind
# PgBouncer in transaction-pooling mode, where each transaction may get a
# different backend and a cached statement handle refers to something that
# backend has never prepared.
# POSTGRES_STATEMENT_CACHE=100

# Optional — where the bgutil PO-token provider sidecar listens (docker-compose
# service `bgutil-pot-provider`). The default matches the plugin's built-in default;
# only set this when the provider lives somewhere other than localhost:4416.
# POT_PROVIDER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4416

# Optional — the debug-mode default for servers that have never chosen one. A
# server's `-debug --enable`/`--disable` is DURABLE: it persists to
# guild:{id}:config and wins over this value from then on, across restarts. So
# changing this moves every server that never ran the command, and none that did;
# a server that opted out stays out when you turn this on. Debug mode is
# observation-only: replies gain a footer
# carrying the trace id, elapsed time and live runtime metrics, and nothing else
# changes. Safe to leave on; it is off by default because the footer is noise for
# ordinary users. Strict parse — true/1/yes or false/0/no, and a typo aborts startup
# rather than silently reading as off.
# DEBUG_MODE=false

# Where `-debug` reads the postgres container's CPU/memory from. Compose defaults
# it to http://localhost:9090 (otel-lgtm's Prometheus, published on loopback) and
# honours whatever you set here, so point it elsewhere when the metrics stack lives
# on another host. Unset, the cpu/mem rows render `n/a (no metrics source)` and
# nothing else changes — the block's load/throughput/mem-signal rows are native
# SQL over the archive's own connection and render regardless.
# DEBUG_PROMETHEUS_URL=http://localhost:9090
#
# Host-side port for that Prometheus. Change it if something on this machine
# already owns 9090 — a collision fails the whole `docker compose up`, not just
# the metrics row. DEBUG_PROMETHEUS_URL's default follows this value.
# PROMETHEUS_HOST_PORT=9090
#
# The numbers come from the `otelcol-metrics` sidecar, which is OPT-IN via the
# `metrics` compose profile because it mounts the Docker socket — root-equivalent
# on the host, since `:ro` restricts the file and not the API.
#
# Turn it on by adding `metrics` to the COMPOSE_PROFILES list in your shell
# environment, or per command: `docker compose --profile metrics up -d`. Deliberately
# NOT a commented-out assignment here — `.env` is copied from this file, and the
# archive tier derives its own profile from HISTORY_ARCHIVE_ENABLED, so a
# ready-to-uncomment profile line is how that single switch becomes two again.
# The deploy scripts own only the `archive` element of the list and leave the rest
# alone, so a `metrics` you set there survives every deploy.
#
# Without it the stack runs normally, minus the dashboard's per-container panels
# and -debug's postgres cpu/mem rows (which read `n/a (no metrics source)`; the
# block's native SQL rows stay live either way).

# `-debug`'s live-edit loop, the same shape as PING_TICK_SECS/PING_DEADLINE_SECS.
# The snapshot is sent immediately and its blocks fill in as their IO lands; a block
# that has not answered by the deadline renders `timed out` and the rest of the card
# is unaffected. The tick is a ceiling on how long the card can be stale, not a
# polling interval — the loop wakes as soon as a block is ready.
# DEBUG_TICK_SECS=1.0
# DEBUG_DEADLINE_SECS=8.0

# Observability (OpenTelemetry)
OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=false
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=discord-music-bot
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317
